{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"agent_message","text":"Very interesting! For B=2:\n- Input projection: einsum is **2x faster** than matmul (6.5ms vs 12.2ms)\n- Output projection: einsum is **4x faster** than matmul (2.4ms vs 10.7ms)\n- Value projection: einsum is faster than broadcast matmul\n\nThe issue is that `x.unsqueeze(1) @ wq.transpose(-1,-2)` does a broadcast matmul with batch dims (B, 1) and (H,) → broadcasting to (B, H). This creates a lot of work that einsum handles more efficiently for small B and small H.\n\nWait, but with B=1, matmul was faster. Let me re-check:\n</think>Critical finding: matmul is faster than einsum for B=1 cases but much slower for B>1 due to broadcasting overhead. I need a strategy that works for all batch sizes. Let me test an approach using reshaped `bmm` or using einsum only for input/output projections but keeping the value projection einsum optimization.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 -c \\\"\nimport time, math\nimport torch\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\n# Test both B=1 and B=2\nfor B, L, C, H, D, label in [(1, 256, 1280, 20, 64, 'B=1 large'),\n                              (2, 128, 512, 8, 64, 'B=2 med'),\n                              (1, 64, 512, 8, 64, 'B=1 small'),\n                              (1, 128, 1280, 20, 64, 'B=1 med-large')]:\n    x = torch.randn(B, L, C)\n    wq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n\n    def time_step(name, fn, n=11):\n        fn()\n        times = []\n        for _ in range(n):\n            s = time.process_time_ns()\n            fn()\n            times.append((time.process_time_ns() - s) / 1e6)\n        return sorted(times)[5]\n\n    # Strategy 1: reshape to (B, H*D, C) and do bmm with x reshaped\n    # Actually: x is (B, L, C), wq is (H, D, C)\n    # result: (B, H, L, D)\n    \n    # Option: reshape x to (B*H, L, C) and wq to (B*H, D, C) then bmm\n    # But expanding x costs memory\n    def proj_expand_bmm():\n        x_exp = x.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, H, L, C).reshape(B*H, L, C)\n        wq_exp = wq.expand(B, H, D, C).reshape(B*H, D, C)\n        return (x_exp @ wq_exp.transpose(-1, -2)).reshape(B, H, L, D)\n    \n    # Option: keep einsum for input projection, but use value-proj einsum optimization\n    # Also try: different einsum notation\n    \n    t_einsum = time_step('einsum blc,hdc->bhld', lambda: torch.einsum('blc,hdc->bhld', x, wq))\n    t_matmul = time_step('matmul unsqueeze', lambda: x.unsqueeze(1) @ wq.transpose(-1,-2))\n    t_bmm = time_step('expand + bmm', proj_expand_bmm)\n    \n    print(f'{label}: einsum={t_einsum:.2f}ms, matmul={t_matmul:.2f}ms, bmm={t_bmm:.2f}ms')\n    print(f'  best: {min(t_einsum, t_matmul, t_bmm):.2f}ms')\n\\\" 2>&1\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
