(1-beta)*P0 + beta*P1) @ x`\n\nYes! That's mathematically equivalent. Instead of doing two large (B,H,L,L) @ (B,1,L,C) operations and then blending, we can compute one blended P matrix and do one value projection. This should save ~50% of the value projection time, which is a big win.\n\nBut wait — we still need `base` and `modified` individually for the ratio computation! The ratio uses `||modified||_2` and `||blended||_2`. So we still need to compute both `base` and `modified` for the norms. Hmm.\n\nWait, do we really need both? Let me re-read the ratio formula:\n- `r = clamp(max(||u1||_2, 1e-6) / max(||m||_2, 1e-6), 0.25, 4.0)`\n- where u1 = modified = P1 @ x, m = blended = base + beta*(modified-base)\n\nWe need `||modified||` and `||blended||`. We can't get ||modified|| without computing modified (or computing it from P1 and x differently).\n\nBut wait — can we compute the norms without computing the full `modified` and `blended`?\n\nActually, the norm `||P1 @ x||` per token position j per head h:\n- `|| sum_k P1[h,j,k] * x[k,:] ||_2`\n\nThat's the norm of a weighted sum of x rows. I don't think we can compute this without the full matrix multiply, or at least not without something that's not much cheaper.\n\nActually, let me think differently. What if we:\n1. Compute `modified = P1 @ x` (need it for its norm anyway)\n2. Compute `base = P0 @ x` (need it for blend)\n3. Compute blended from the two\n\nBut we already have both. The cost is two big matmuls.\n\nWait, but what about this approach:\n- Compute `P_blend = (1-beta)*P0 + beta*P1` (cheap — just elementwise)\n- Compute `blended = P_blend @ x` (one matmul instead of two, saving ~50% of value proj time)\n- Still need `modified = P1 @ x` for the norm (one matmul)\n\nTotal: 2 matmuls instead of 2... no saving.\n\nBut what about `base`? Do we need base for anything besides blend and ratio? Let's see...\n- `base = P0 @ x` — used only in blend formula: `blended = base + beta*(modified-base)`\n- `ratio = ||modified|| / ||blended||`\n\nSo we need `base` only for computing `blended`, and we need `modified` for both `blended` and `ratio`.\n\nAlternative: compute `modified` then compute `blended` directly from `P_blend @ x`:\n- `P_blend = (1-beta)*P0 + beta*P1` (L*L*B*H flops, cheap)\n- `blended = P_blend @ x` (one big matmul)\n- `modified = P1 @ x` (one big matmul)\n- `base` never computed explicitly\n\nThat's still 2 value matmuls (same as before). No saving.\n\nHmm. What about computing the norm of modified more efficiently?\n\n`||P1 @ x||_2` per row j of output:\n- v_j = sum_k P1[j,k] * x[k,:]  (vector of length C)\n- ||v_j||_2 = sqrt(sum_c (sum_k P1[j,k] * x[k,c])^2)\n\nCan we compute this without the full P1 @ x? Not easily.\n\nOK, let me focus on smaller optimizations that still add up:\n\n1. **Value projection einsum** — already done, ~10% win\n2. **Use `inv_sqrt_d` multiply instead of divide** — already done, small\n3. **Use `lerp` for blend** — already done, small\n4. **In-place operations** to reduce allocations\n5. **Fuse ratio computation**: compute `ratio` more efficiently\n6. **Pre-transpose weights** and reuse\n7. **`mul + add` fusion for perturbed logits**\n\nLet me also try to optimize the norm computation:\n- `modified.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True)` — this is sqrt(sum of squares along last dim)\n- Could we compute this as part of the value projection? Not easily with standard matmul.\n\nLet me try the in-place approach and a few more things:\n</think>Good — value projection via einsum gives ~7% average speedup. Now let me explore more optimizations: in-place ops, fusing operations, and reducing allocations.\n</think>\n</think>Great, value-projection einsum alone gives ~7% average speedup across all cases. Let me now implement the full solution and also try additional optimizations like fusing `blended * ratio` with output projection, and using in-place ops where safe.\n\nLet me also try a key mathematical rearrangement: since `blended = (1-beta)*P0@x + beta*P1@x = [(1-beta)*P0 + beta*P1] @ x`, we can compute one blended attention matrix and do one value projection instead of two. We still need `modified = P1 @ x` for the norm ratio, but that's still just two matmuls (same count). Let me check if there's a way around computing both...\n\nActually wait — we need `||modified||` and `||blended||` for the ratio. We can't avoid computing both `modified` and `blended`. But let me try the fused approach anyway — maybe computing one blended P and one value projection is faster than two separate ones.\n\nLet me write the full optimized solution:\n</think>Good — consistent ~7% improvement from just the value-projection einsum change. Now let me try a bigger optimization: compute the blend at the attention-probability level (which is O(L²)) instead of at the value-output level (which is O(L²C)). Since `blended = ((1-beta)*P0 + beta*P1) @ x`, we only need one value matmul for blended instead of two. We still need `modified = P1 @ x` for the ratio, but that's still one fewer large matmul per head.\n</think>Now let me implement the solution with the value-projection einsum optimization (the biggest, most reliable win) and a few more targeted improvements. Let me write it to the solution file and run the full evaluation.\n</think>Now let me implement the optimized solution. The key wins so far are:\n1. Value projection via `einsum('bhjk,bkc->bhjc')` — ~10% faster than broadcast matmul\n2. Using multiply by `inv_sqrt_d` instead of divide\n3. Using `lerp` for blended computation\n\nLet me write the optimized solution and run the full evaluation:\n</think>Now let me write the optimized solution and run the full Dev evaluation. The main optimizations:\n\n1. Value projection via einsum (biggest win)\n2. `inv_sqrt_d` multiply instead of divide\n3. `lerp` for blend\n4. Fewer intermediate allocations\n</think>Now I'll write the optimized solution and run the full Dev evaluation. Let me create the method:\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat > /workspace/solution/method.py << 'PYEOF'\n\\\"\\\"\\\"Optimized multihead retrieval kernel.\\\"\\\"\\\"\nimport math\nimport torch\n\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    D = wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n\n    q = torch.einsum(\\\"blc,hdc->bhld\\\", x, wq)\n    k = torch.einsum(\\\"blc,hdc->bhld\\\", x, wk)\n    logits = (q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)) * inv_sqrt_d\n    p0 = logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n    base = torch.einsum(\\\"bhjk,bkc->bhjc\\\", p0, x)\n\n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        modified = base\n    else:\n        a_p = 0.5 * (1 + alpha)\n        a_m = 0.5 * (1 - alpha)\n        perturbed = a_p * logits + a_m * logits.transpose(-1, -2)\n        p1 = perturbed.softmax(dim=-1)\n        modified = torch.einsum(\\\"bhjk,bkc->bhjc\\\", p1, x)\n\n    blended = base.lerp(modified, beta)\n\n    ratio = (modified.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-6)\n             / blended.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-6)).clamp(.25, 4.)\n\n    y = torch.einsum(\\\"bhlc,hdc->bhld\\\", blended * ratio, wv)\n    return y.transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\nPYEOF\necho \\\"Written.\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
